Subject: [PATCH] a bot needs on loot it has already decided it cannot equip

WHY THIS PATCH EXISTS

The operator's ask, in his words: "They should greed roll on things that are
greed and need on others when it's truly a need" (mod-overseer#10). The proven
counter-example, from a real drop: a green two-handed Severing Axe rolled, and
UGGA THE PRIEST won it with a NEED roll. She can never equip a two-handed axe.
It has sat in her bags ever since (mod-overseer#14 counts seven such weapons).

That is not a scoring near-miss or a talent-weighting subtlety. The bot had
ALREADY correctly worked out it could not use the item, and the loot code threw
that answer away.

THE MECHANISM, TRACED AT THIS PIN

ItemUsageValue::QueryItemUsageForEquip (src/Ai/Base/Value/ItemUsageValue.cpp)
decides equippability BY CLASS before it scores anything:

    if (itemProto->Class == ITEM_CLASS_WEAPON &&
        !sRandomItemMgr.CanEquipWeapon(itemProto, bot->getClass()))
        shouldEquip = false;                                       (~line 235)

A priest offered a two-handed axe lands here: shouldEquip = false. The very
next block turns that into a return value:

    Item* oldItem = bot->GetItemByPos(dest + i);
    if (!oldItem)
    {
        if (shouldEquipInSlot)  return ITEM_USAGE_EQUIP;
        else                    return ITEM_USAGE_BAD_EQUIP;       (~line 297)
    }

So ITEM_USAGE_BAD_EQUIP is the value that means "this bot should NOT equip
this item" - and it covers BOTH "wrong class entirely" and "equippable but
does not clear equipUpgradeThreshold". Every other consumer treats it that
way; BuyAction, for one, groups it with BROKEN_EQUIP.

LootRollAction did not:

    case ITEM_CLASS_WEAPON:
    case ITEM_CLASS_ARMOR:
        if (usage == ITEM_USAGE_EQUIP || usage == ITEM_USAGE_REPLACE ||
            usage == ITEM_USAGE_BAD_EQUIP)
            vote = NEED;                                           (line 67)

BAD_EQUIP - the "I cannot use this" answer - voted NEED. LootRollAction::
CalculateRollVote had the identical bug, listing ITEM_USAGE_BAD_EQUIP in the
same case group as EQUIP and REPLACE (line 127).

THE FIX

Both sites now treat BAD_EQUIP as GREED. In Execute() removing it from the
NEED condition is sufficient: BAD_EQUIP != ITEM_USAGE_NONE, so it falls to the
existing `else if (usage != ITEM_USAGE_NONE) vote = GREED;` on the next line.
In CalculateRollVote it moves into the GREED case group.

This is exactly mod-overseer#10's stated rule - NEED is equippable AND an
upgrade, GREED is everything else - and it makes the Severing Axe test in
mod-overseer#14 pass: the axe becomes a greed for Ugga, so it goes to whoever
greeds highest instead of being locked behind a need she should never have won.

WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT CHANGE

An item in an EMPTY slot that the bot's class CAN equip but that the stat
calculator scores at zero also returns BAD_EQUIP, and now greeds rather than
needs. That is correct under #10's rule (a zero-weight item is not a need) and
it still gets won - GREED is a real roll, not a pass, while LootGreedRollLevel
is 1, which is what this realm runs.

RollAction::Execute (line ~240) has the same three-way test but only calls
DoRandomRoll for a /roll link a human asked for. Left alone on purpose - it is
not a group loot vote and needs its own decision.

WHAT WOULD LET THIS PATCH BE DELETED

Upstream making BAD_EQUIP not a NEED in LootRollAction, or splitting the enum
so "wrong class" and "equippable but not an upgrade" stop sharing one value.

APPLIES TO

mod-playerbots @ 8d9f6aa6bc6d45f9ae0ee0675b9b1f8aa6937312, on top of local
patches 0001-0007. Touches only LootRollAction::Execute and
LootRollAction::CalculateRollVote.

